 "facts": [
  {
   "claim": "On 2 July 1816 the French frigate Méduse ran aground on a sandbank off the West African coast, near today's Mauritania",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The ship carried 400 people but the boats held only about 250; the rest (at least 146 men and one woman; Louvre: 150 people) were piled onto a hastily built raft",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa ; https://www.louvre.fr/en/explore/visitor-trails/the-louvre-s-masterpieces/romanticism-topicality-sensuality"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The boats intended to tow the raft, but after only a few miles the raft was turned loose",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa (citing Borias)"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The raft drifted for 13 days; the survivors slaughtered mutineers, killed the weakest and ate their dead companions; cannibalism",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa (citing Jonathan Miles, The Times, 2007; Riding 2003) ; https://www.louvre.fr/en/explore/visitor-trails/the-louvre-s-masterpieces/romanticism-topicality-sensuality"
  },
  {
   "claim": "When the Argus found the raft on 17 July 1816, only 15 men were alive",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa ; https://www.louvre.fr/en/explore/visitor-trails/the-louvre-s-masterpieces/romanticism-topicality-sensuality"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In early 1818 Géricault met two survivors, Henri Savigny and Alexandre Corréard, and built a detailed scale model of the raft with them (and the carpenter Lavillette)",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa ; https://www.louvre.fr/en/explore/visitor-trails/the-louvre-s-masterpieces/romanticism-topicality-sensuality (interviewed survivors, built scale models)"
  },
  {
   "claim": "He sketched bodies in the morgue of the Hôpital Beaujon and brought severed limbs back to his studio to study their decay; his studies of severed heads and limbs (Nationalmuseum Stockholm, Musée Fabre) are linked to the Raft",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa (citing Miles p. 169) ; https://hyperallergic.com/331446/corpse-models-raft-medusa-gericault/ ; https://collection.nationalmuseum.se/en/collection/item/19173/ ; https://www.louvre.fr/en/explore/visitor-trails/the-louvre-s-masterpieces/romanticism-topicality-sensuality (visited morgues)"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Account (Rupert Christiansen) that he kept a severed head borrowed from a lunatic asylum on his studio roof for a fortnight; narrated as 'one account says'",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa (citing Christiansen, NYT 2001) ; https://hyperallergic.com/331446/corpse-models-raft-medusa-gericault/"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Shown at the 1819 Paris Salon; critics were divided; classicists dismissed it as a 'pile of corpses'",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa ; https://www.louvre.fr/en/explore/visitor-trails/the-louvre-s-masterpieces/romanticism-topicality-sensuality (critics captivated or repelled)"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The Louvre bought it in 1824 after Géricault's death; it hangs in the Louvre, Paris (Denon wing, room 700)",
   "source": "https://www.louvre.fr/en/explore/visitor-trails/the-louvre-s-masterpieces/romanticism-topicality-sensuality ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa"
  }
 ],
 "caption": "On 2 July 1816 the French frigate Méduse ran aground off West Africa. There was room in the boats for about 250 of the 400 on board, so around 150 people were piled onto a raft. The boats were meant to tow it. After a few miles, they let it go.\n\nFor 13 days the raft drifted. The men fought, killed the weakest and ate the dead. When the ship Argus found them, only 15 were still alive.\n\nIn 1818 the young painter Théodore Géricault met two of the survivors and built a scale model of the raft with them. Then he sketched bodies at the morgue of the Hôpital Beaujon and took severed limbs back to his studio to paint them as they decayed. One account says he kept a severed head on his studio roof for two weeks.\n\nAt the 1819 Salon the critics were split: some were gripped, others called it a \"pile of corpses\". Géricault died in 1824, aged 32, and the Louvre bought the painting that year.\n\n🖤 Could you look at it for long?",
 "caption_short": "To paint The Raft of the Medusa, Géricault took severed arms and legs home from the morgue. 🖤",
